WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.139 --> 00:00:08.090
Even the most rugged and hardiest of men cry
sometimes. There’s no escaping the tear.

00:00:08.090 --> 00:00:12.969
In fact, studies suggest that you probably
cry a lot more than you think you do. During

00:00:12.969 --> 00:00:17.980
a study by the German Society of Ophthalmology,
they found that women cry on average between

00:00:17.980 --> 00:00:24.039
thirty to sixty-four times per year, whereas
men cry between six and seventeen times per

00:00:24.039 --> 00:00:25.039
year.

00:00:25.039 --> 00:00:30.789
Women are actually biologically hard-wired
to be more susceptible to crying. Women have

00:00:30.789 --> 00:00:36.800
sixty-percent more prolactin in their bodies
than men. Prolactin is a reproductive hormone

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:42.050
which is also found in tears and is partially
responsible for women being more prone to

00:00:42.050 --> 00:00:47.989
tearing up. On the other hand, testosterone,
a hormone which is more abundant in males,

00:00:47.989 --> 00:00:49.379
inhibits crying.

00:00:49.379 --> 00:00:54.489
However, this difference in the frequency
of tears shed between genders, doesn’t come

00:00:54.489 --> 00:01:00.780
into effect until the age of thirteen. Before
they are thirteen, both girls and boys cry

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:02.570
the same amount.

00:01:02.570 --> 00:01:08.880
Men and women don’t just differ in how frequently
they cry; they also cry for different durations.

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:14.390
Studies show that men tend to cry for between
two to four minutes, whereas women cry for

00:01:14.390 --> 00:01:16.520
an average of six minutes.

00:01:16.520 --> 00:01:21.420
Maybe this is why in almost every culture
on Earth it is more socially acceptable for

00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:28.729
Women to cry than Men. Certain cultures actually
shun crying altogether, for both genders,

00:01:28.729 --> 00:01:34.860
seeing it as undignified and infantile. Despite
the negative connotations they may carry,

00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:40.460
tears are most certainly, a force for good,
they can call others to help you and even

00:01:40.460 --> 00:01:48.850
heal you from within. But why do humans cry
in the first place? Let’s find out.

00:01:48.850 --> 00:01:55.799
They may all look the same on the surface,
but there are three different types of tears,

00:01:55.799 --> 00:02:01.759
and they’re all quite unique. The first
kind of tear is basal tears. In all mammals

00:02:01.759 --> 00:02:07.100
the cornea of the eye is continually kept
wet by basal tears. Our eyes are some of the

00:02:07.100 --> 00:02:11.840
most vulnerable and sensitive parts of our
outer body, so it’s incredibly important

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:17.230
that they are protected at all times. The
basal tears ensure our eyes are well protected

00:02:17.230 --> 00:02:19.160
from bacteria and irritants.

00:02:19.160 --> 00:02:24.110
Basal tears are made up of three distinct
layers. The first layer which sits directly

00:02:24.110 --> 00:02:29.200
on the eye’s surface, is the mucus layer.
This helps the tear to spread out and coat

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:33.300
the entire eyeball, it also keeps the eye
moisturised.

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:39.540
The next, the middle layer, is the aqueous
layer, consisting mostly of water. It contains

00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:45.150
vitamins and nutrients, keeping the cornea
healthy. This layer also contains salt which

00:02:45.150 --> 00:02:48.930
acts as an antibacterial. This is why tears
taste salty.

00:02:48.930 --> 00:02:55.670
And the third, outermost layer is the lipid
layer. This layer, made up entirely of oils,

00:02:55.670 --> 00:03:01.069
acts as a sealant to stop your tears from
evaporating and keeps bacteria and other irritants

00:03:01.069 --> 00:03:07.409
out. This layer also makes your vision sharp.
The convex of this oily film which coats your

00:03:07.409 --> 00:03:13.470
cornea creates an incredibly smooth surface.
This helps to smooth out the natural bumps

00:03:13.470 --> 00:03:19.000
and fissures on your cornea which can cause
blurred vision. This is why people get blurry

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:21.550
vision when they have dry eyes.

00:03:21.550 --> 00:03:27.129
The second type of tear is the reflex tear.
These are only released when foreign particles

00:03:27.129 --> 00:03:32.750
make contact with the eye which could irritate
or even damage the eye. For example, pepper

00:03:32.750 --> 00:03:40.140
spray, tear gas, capsicum and of course onion
vapors. When these potentially harmful substances

00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:44.519
make contact with the cornea, reflex tears
are produced to wash them away.

00:03:44.519 --> 00:03:51.430
And finally, the last kind of tear is what
has perplexed scientists for so long, psychogenic

00:03:51.430 --> 00:03:56.909
tears. When we talk about someone crying,
sobbing or weeping, we are usually referring

00:03:56.909 --> 00:04:02.180
to psychogenic tears. These are tears that
are caused by strong emotions and physical

00:04:02.180 --> 00:04:07.620
pain. It might be stresses at work or at home.
A family member passing away or perhaps a

00:04:07.620 --> 00:04:11.459
pet. Or maybe a painful breakup with a loved
one.

00:04:11.459 --> 00:04:17.239
But psychogenic tears aren’t exclusively
caused by negative emotions. We can also turn

00:04:17.239 --> 00:04:23.090
on the waterworks when we’re overwhelmingly
happy or during an intense bout of laughter.

00:04:23.090 --> 00:04:29.020
That covers all the different types of tears,
but all tears originate from the same place.

00:04:29.020 --> 00:04:34.860
All human tears are formed in the lacrimal
gland; resembling a raspberry, it sits just

00:04:34.860 --> 00:04:40.160
above your eyeball. It may be small in size
but it’s easily one of the most complex

00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:45.949
systems in the human body. Tears are formed
inside it, then the small bulbs that make

00:04:45.949 --> 00:04:52.080
up its unique shape, contract to pump the
tears downwards, exiting through little tubes

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:54.830
and ending up on your eyeball.

00:04:54.830 --> 00:05:00.449
Your blinking is actually synchronised with
your lacrimal glands. When a new tear is released

00:05:00.449 --> 00:05:06.630
from the gland, whether it be a basal, reflex
or psychogenic tear, you automatically and

00:05:06.630 --> 00:05:12.660
subconsciously blink. This blinking motion
spreads the new tear evenly across the surface

00:05:12.660 --> 00:05:13.750
of your eye.

00:05:13.750 --> 00:05:18.789
Once a tear has covered your eyeball it has
two possible exit routes. The first is the

00:05:18.789 --> 00:05:24.080
standard route, down the lacrimal punctum.
Think of this as the drain on your kitchen

00:05:24.080 --> 00:05:30.710
sink. There are two lacrimal punctum in each
eye. They are located on the inside of your

00:05:30.710 --> 00:05:36.289
eyelids. In fact, if you’re so inclined,
these tiny little drainage pumps can be seen

00:05:36.289 --> 00:05:40.139
with the naked eye if you look really, really
closely.

00:05:40.139 --> 00:05:45.880
Their sole job is to drain tears from your
cornea once the tear has served its purpose.

00:05:45.880 --> 00:05:51.130
The tears that make their way down the lacrimal
punctum are drained down through your nose.

00:05:51.130 --> 00:05:54.530
Which is why your nose gets stuffy when you
cry.

00:05:54.530 --> 00:06:00.350
The second possible escape route for a tear
is of course down your cheek and then if you’ve

00:06:00.350 --> 00:06:04.080
just gone through a break up, probably straight
into a tub of ice cream.

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:10.470
What happens when you fill the sink up too
high and it’s at risk of overflowing? The

00:06:10.470 --> 00:06:15.110
water goes through the overflow. That’s
exactly what happens when you produce tears

00:06:15.110 --> 00:06:20.479
faster than your lacrimal punctum can drain
them away. When the lacrimal punctum gets

00:06:20.479 --> 00:06:27.190
overloaded, the excess tears have nowhere
else to go, and so they roll down your cheeks.

00:06:27.190 --> 00:06:32.340
There is a system in your brain specifically
designed for dealing with emotions, it’s

00:06:32.340 --> 00:06:38.430
called the limbic system. When we get emotional
the limbic system is activated. The limbic

00:06:38.430 --> 00:06:44.030
system is hard-wired with our autonomic nervous
system, which is the control centre for the

00:06:44.030 --> 00:06:49.870
function of our internal organs. The autonomic
nervous system is connected to and can control

00:06:49.870 --> 00:06:56.410
every major organ in your body and thereby
the limbic system can influence and use this

00:06:56.410 --> 00:07:02.590
immense power. This is how emotions can effect
us physically, for instance, emotions can

00:07:02.590 --> 00:07:07.410
cause shaky and clammy hands, an increased
heart rate, sweating, stomach cramps and of

00:07:07.410 --> 00:07:08.690
course crying.

00:07:08.690 --> 00:07:15.110
But what’s the point in tears? Why is it
that when we get upset, the limbic system

00:07:15.110 --> 00:07:21.120
in our brain feels it necessary to alert our
autonomic nervous system, to command the lacrimal

00:07:21.120 --> 00:07:27.370
glands above our eyes to start rapidly producing
these weird, oily, salty drops of water?

00:07:27.370 --> 00:07:32.349
Just like most human functions and behaviours,
there’s usually a solid, evolutionary reason

00:07:32.349 --> 00:07:36.849
for having said function. If humans can do
something, it’s usually because there’s

00:07:36.849 --> 00:07:42.949
a bloody good reason for it, or at least there
used to be. In this respect crying is no different

00:07:42.949 --> 00:07:49.199
than most other human abilities; it’s believed
crying has its roots in evolution also. For

00:07:49.199 --> 00:07:54.360
any ability to have survived the ruthless
critic that is evolution, it must, in some

00:07:54.360 --> 00:08:00.120
way, be beneficial to our survival. But what
are the survival benefits of crying?

00:08:00.120 --> 00:08:05.430
Well, there are actually two major benefits
of turning on the waterworks, which explain

00:08:05.430 --> 00:08:10.530
why we have the ability to cry in the first
place. The first benefit is that it makes

00:08:10.530 --> 00:08:18.300
us feel better. Get this, we don’t actually
cry because we feel sad. We cry to get over

00:08:18.300 --> 00:08:24.840
being sad. Crying is our body’s own moral
booster and healer of mental wounds. 90% of

00:08:24.840 --> 00:08:28.710
people report feeling happier and less stressed
after crying.

00:08:28.710 --> 00:08:35.680
When you cry and someone says “let it out”,
they really mean it, because when we cry we

00:08:35.680 --> 00:08:41.810
are quite literally crying our stresses away.
Stress-causing chemicals that build up when

00:08:41.810 --> 00:08:47.720
we’re under emotional tension are physically
removed from the body in our tears when we

00:08:47.720 --> 00:08:53.290
cry. Crying also stimulates the release of
endorphins in the brain. Endorphins are the

00:08:53.290 --> 00:08:56.790
happy hormone, they relieve stress and lift-up
your mood.

00:08:56.790 --> 00:09:02.890
The second benefit of crying is communication.
Before humans developed language, some of

00:09:02.890 --> 00:09:08.630
the only forms of communication we had, were
basic noises, such as laughter and sobbing.

00:09:08.630 --> 00:09:13.360
When our ancient ancestors hurt themselves
or were feeling upset, the sight of tears

00:09:13.360 --> 00:09:18.130
rolling down their face will have spurred
their cohorts to come to their aid. This exact

00:09:18.130 --> 00:09:23.250
same behavior can still be seen in babies
today. Since babies can’t talk they use

00:09:23.250 --> 00:09:29.339
tears to communicate when something is wrong,
whether it be pain, hunger or tiredness. Or

00:09:29.339 --> 00:09:31.420
perhaps they’re just missing their mummy.

00:09:31.420 --> 00:09:36.390
Crying works so well to attract another’s
attention, because humans are genetically

00:09:36.390 --> 00:09:42.750
programmed to feel sympathy for people who
are in tears. Psychologists demonstrated this

00:09:42.750 --> 00:09:48.339
in a study where they digitally removed tears
from images of people crying and monitored

00:09:48.339 --> 00:09:54.370
people’s sympathetic response. All the participants
judged the people in the images with tears

00:09:54.370 --> 00:09:57.779
to be sadder than in the image without tears.

00:09:57.779 --> 00:10:02.720
Studies show that crying is also good for
your health. A good sob lowers your blood

00:10:02.720 --> 00:10:07.860
pressure and removes unwanted toxins from
your body. There has even been evidence that

00:10:07.860 --> 00:10:13.070
crying can help to regulate your immune system;
in particular, during a study, it was found

00:10:13.070 --> 00:10:18.440
that people who have recently had a good cry
were less susceptible to allergic reactions

00:10:18.440 --> 00:10:19.920
and eczema.

00:10:19.920 --> 00:10:25.959
Crying is one of the most basic yet powerful
forms of communication we possess. It can

00:10:25.959 --> 00:10:31.880
heal your mind and your body, relieve stresses
and bring people closer together and there’s

00:10:31.880 --> 00:10:35.519
certainly no shame in that.

